phpBugTracker "2025" UPGRADING
==============================

If you are still on PHP 5.x, phpBugTracker 2025 can still be used.

o Download the latest release zip file
o Unzip the zipfile in a new location from your old location.
o Edit and run setperms.sh
o Copy config-dist.php to config.php and copy values from your config.php
  - set your database connection parameters
  - set your SMTP settings if being used.

There has been a change to one of phpBugTrackers table names to prevent a 
reserved word conflict when the Oracle database is used.

There are two ways to fix this in your upgrade:

  1. Change the table alias name in config.php:
        //define ('TBL_BUG_COMMENT',     TBL_PREFIX.'bug_comment');
        define ('TBL_BUG_COMMENT',     TBL_PREFIX.'comment');
   -or-
  2. Make the change permanent in the database. Rename the table:
     ex: for MySQL:
       mysql> RENAME TABLE comment TO bug_comment;

It would be wise to create a copy of your live database and perform
the above changes on the copy and test phpBugTracker with your PHP 5.

Composer Packages
-----------------
PHP 5 and Composer packages can be finicky depending on your versions
of PHP, Composer, and PEAR package dependencies availability. Particularly
phpmailer/phpmailer and pear/spreadsheet_excel_writer.

For PHP < 5.6 will require a downgraded Composer to 2.2.x in order to
install the phpmailer/phpmailer package as older versions of 
phpmailer does not have any autoload ability whereas PHP >= 5.6
may not need to be downgraded and will install a version of 
phpmailer/phpmailer with an autoload ability. There is a changeover
right in that timeframe whether it had an autoload class loader
or not.

The pear/spreadsheet_excel_writer, which allows you to export bug
reports to Excel, may have difficulties in resolving all its 
dependencies as these are very old and not all the old versions
made it into the packagist.org packages. At least with PHP 5.4.7
this is a problem and not installable. YMMV on this.

Best course of action is just start with your current Composer version
and try installing the PEAR packages and observe what Composer comes
back with for errors, if any:

   $ # start anew
   $ mv vendor vendor-dist                 # save originals
   $ mv composer.json composer.json-dist   # save originals
   $ mv composer.lock composer.lock-dist   # save originals
   $ composer clear-cache
   $ composer require pear/db
   $ composer require phpmailer/phpmailer
   $ composer require mitoteam/jpgraph
   $ composer require pear/spreadsheet_excel_writer  # YMMV

